## Advanced 3D Ghostface Nail Art

A flat, painted Ghostface mask is the entry point. Advanced sets take the design off the nail surface entirely, and this is where the more experienced side of the audience is spending their Halloween budget this year.

3D Ghostface work typically means sculpted acrylic elements, raised eye sockets, a built-out robe hood shape, or even a tiny sculpted knife charm sitting beside the mask. Nail artists build these on gel-X or acrylic extensions rather than natural nails, since the added length and rigidity give the sculpting something to hold onto. The process starts the same way as the flat version, sketching the mask's proportions, but instead of stopping at paint, a small amount of acrylic powder is built up over the eye and mouth areas before the final black detailing goes on top. This creates actual shadow and depth instead of the illusion of it.

Chrome powder is doing heavy lifting in the most-saved advanced designs this season, buffed over silver or gunmetal polish around the mask to catch light without competing with the black-and-white focal point. Some sets go further and add a tiny sculpted 3D knife charm to the accent nail, a small but consistent detail across the most-liked TikTok tutorials in this category. This level of detail is genuinely time-intensive, often ninety minutes or more per set at a salon, and it's not the place to start if this is your first attempt at nail art.

