## How to Do Scream Nails at Home, Step by Step

<strong>Scream nails</strong> at home take roughly 45 minutes to an hour for a single accent nail design, done from start to finish with basic polish and a fine liner brush. Here's the exact process nail artists are using in this season's most-followed tutorials.

Start with a clean, shaped nail and one coat of base coat to protect the nail bed. Once cured or dried, apply two coats of black polish or gel across whichever nails will carry the design, typically all ten if the mask is only on one accent nail, or a black base across the whole set if you're doing a more coordinated look. Let this fully dry or cure before moving on, since any tackiness will drag the white detailing.

For the mask itself, a **stamping plate technique** offers the most consistent results for anyone without freehand experience. A stamping plate is a small etched metal plate carrying a pre-cut Ghostface design, used by picking up polish from the plate with a rubber stamper and transferring it directly onto the nail in one motion. Press white polish into the etched design, scrape the excess with a scraper card, then press the stamper onto the plate and immediately onto the nail before the polish dries. This takes seconds per nail and produces the same clean result every time.

Freehand artists skip the stamp and go straight in with a dotting tool to lightly mark the rough shape of the eyes and mouth in white polish first, then fill in with a fine liner brush once that guide layer is dry. Black detailing goes on last: the eye outlines, the mouth shape, and any shadow work around the edges of the mask. Finish with two coats of a high-gloss or matte top coat depending on which finish you're going for, and cure under a UV lamp if you're working with gel.

